{
  "thinking": "Competing explanations: One oversized run was split across job labels or clusters; check sealed job timing, cross-cluster traffic, and metered load. Several independent customers ran smaller jobs at the same time; check customer records, payments, and whether their GPUs exchanged traffic. Flat batch inference resembled training; compare GPU-to-GPU traffic, outbound traffic, and live chip readings where available. An unlisted cluster hosted training; compare manufacturer inventory with the operator's system list and reconcile site energy. Missing logs or late payments created apparent discrepancies; compare current logs with sealed copies and check payment dates independently. Start with the manufacturer's inventory for the system map.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Competing explanations and checks: (1) Separate legitimate training runs stayed below 50,000 GPU-hours. Check escrowed allocations, customer identities, and cross-cluster traffic. (2) Sustained demand came from inference. Compare outbound traffic and GPU-to-GPU traffic, then use live chip probes where available. (3) One coordinated training run was split across job labels or clusters. Use balance checks to flag matching allocations, then test for cross-cluster coordination with the independent tap. (4) Unlisted GPUs hosted a large training run. Compare the manufacturer inventory with the operator's list and test discrepancies against grid energy and feed spectra. (5) Missing logs or late payments explain record gaps. Compare escrow with current logs and bank records with independent customer checks.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "I’ll test five explanations. One oversized run may be split across labels or clusters. I’ll compare sealed job histories with independent interconnect samples and check whether the linked GPU-hours exceed 50,000. Several legitimate runs may overlap. I’ll check customer identities and compare job timing with interconnect evidence. Flat demand or periodic power may come from batch inference or cooling. I’ll compare power spectra with interconnect traffic, ISP records and live chip samples where available. Missing logs or late payments may be ordinary recordkeeping gaps. I’ll compare scheduler records with escrow and check payment timing against bank records. A run may use unlisted hardware. I’ll start with manufacturer inventory, compare it with the operator’s system list and check for unexplained grid energy.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "A single large run may be split across labels or clusters. Check sealed logs for overlapping allocations, then test for coordination with the interconnect tap. Multiple independent customers may have run legitimate jobs concurrently. Check customer registrations and replies, and sample traffic between clusters. Flat batch inference may resemble training. Compare outbound traffic and interconnect measurements, with live chip probes where available. A run may have used unlisted GPUs. Compare manufacturer inventory with the operator's system list, then reconcile the grid energy. Missing logs or late payments may reflect ordinary recordkeeping gaps. Compare the live records with escrow and bank records before treating gaps as evidence. Start with manufacturer inventory for the system map.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The manufacturer records 2,000 GPUs delivered to Eemshaven. Compare that shipment with the operator’s cluster counts, serial ranges, and feed assignments to identify any gaps that need independent checks.",
  "action": "system_list",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Compare the operator’s system list with the manufacturer’s record of 2,000 delivered GPUs. Any discrepancy is a lead to test against independent evidence of site activity.",
  "action": "system_list",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The escrow confirms that all 30 daily copies match the current scheduler records. I’ll read the job entries to identify allocations that could exceed 50,000 GPU-hours, including overlapping jobs on A and B, then use independent interconnect measurements to test whether those jobs were coordinated.",
  "action": "scheduler_log",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The grid records show temporary load increases on F1 on days 4–6 and 15–16 and on F2 on days 9–10. The steady load on other days could also conceal a longer run. Check the sealed scheduler records for allocations, job continuity, and overlaps across both clusters, then use those leads to target independent measurements.",
  "action": "log_escrow",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Grid readings show a steady baseline and short increases on F1 on days 4–6 and 15–16, and on F2 on days 9–10. Compare all 30 days with the internet provider’s outbound traffic to see whether user-facing inference tracks those loads. Sustained load with little outbound traffic would leave both batch inference and training plausible.",
  "action": "outbound_traffic",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Escrow establishes what the scheduler recorded on days 11–13 despite the missing operator copies. Those days sit within a stretch of steady grid load. Check for GPU-to-GPU traffic on day 12 using the verifier’s tap. Heavy traffic would support unrecorded training, while light traffic would leave ordinary inference and low-communication training open.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 12
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The two Cluster A fine-tunes overlap on day 8. The grid meter supports concurrent GPU use, but separate customers can train at the same time. Run the fixed balance checks to verify the recorded GPU-hours and reconcile allocations with metered load. The day 8 interconnect is the next lead for testing whether the two allocations worked together.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "On day 12, the sealed log records only interactive inference, but Cluster A’s sampled GPU traffic is about 1,000 times the typical inference rate and matches low-communication training. Check F1 for a training-like power line that could corroborate the tap. A line alone could also come from inference or cooling, and its absence would leave intermittent training possible.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 12
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The sealed copies match the current logs for all 30 days, so the scheduler can identify candidate jobs for independent checks. Separate legitimate runs would be tested through customer records and cross-cluster traffic. Batch inference would be tested through interconnect traffic and live chip probes. A training run split across labels would be tested through allocation overlap and the independent tap. Unlisted capacity would be tested against manufacturer inventory and grid readings. Late payments would be checked against bank and customer records.",
  "action": "scheduler_log",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The two Cluster A allocations total 50,400 recorded GPU-hours, so the verdict hinges on whether they were part of one training process. Sample Cluster A on day 8, when both allocations were active, to look for traffic between the two node ranges. High aggregate traffic alone would also fit two independent fine-tunes.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 8
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Use the fixed balance checks to see whether the three recorded jobs account for the independently metered loads and whether any sustained activity remains unexplained. Use any discrepancy to target an independent test of workload type and coordination.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 8 tap measured 647 GB/s across Cluster A, consistent with both independent fine-tunes and one coordinated run. The two allocations total 50,400 recorded GPU-hours, so check Tidewater Robotics’ registered ownership and ask whether it commissioned and controlled its fine-tune independently.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Tidewater Robotics"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A’s day 12 traffic is consistent with low-communication training. The missing power line leaves variable training possible, and one day of sampled traffic establishes neither GPU count nor duration. Use the fixed balance checks to see whether the recorded workloads account for the metered energy across all 30 days and to identify where independent measurements could best test for a sustained run.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "F1 showed no periodic line on day 12. Baseline inference remains plausible, and training with a weak or intermittent line remains possible. Sample Cluster A's GPU-to-GPU traffic on the same day to test those explanations independently of the scheduler and power sensor.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 12
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The customer replies support two separate orders, but the day 8 tap measured only aggregate traffic. The billing check confirms that every invoice was paid. Review the bank records for who paid each invoice and whether the payments came from distinct customers or a common payer.",
  "action": "bank_payments",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The declared jobs are all below the threshold, and none covers Cluster A on day 12. The tap reading that day fits low-communication training, but one sample cannot establish the workload's duration or size. Sample Cluster A on day 28, another day with steady F1 energy and no declared scheduled job. A similar reading would show that the unusual traffic extends beyond the logging gap and give us a live window for a chip probe.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {"link": "A", "day": 28}
}

{
  "thinking": "The ISP saw baseline outbound traffic on days 12–18 and a single spike on day 19. Site-wide traffic cannot identify Meridian’s workload. I’ll sample Cluster A on day 12 to see whether the low-communication training signature observed on day 16 was already present at the start of the allocation.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 12
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A’s day 12 traffic was far above typical inference and much closer to low-communication training, even allowing for sampling error. Sample A again on day 26, another day with no scheduled job, to test whether the anomaly recurs and identify a day when a live chip probe could help classify the workload.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 26
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A’s traffic on days 12 and 28 fits low-communication training, and day 28 has a sealed record with no scheduled job. Probe GPUs across both of A’s declared pools during day 28, with B as a comparison. The results can help distinguish training from atypical inference on the chips presented. They cannot establish run membership, GPU count, or duration.",
  "action": "chip_probe",
  "args": {
    "nodes": ["A-0050", "A-0250", "A-0450", "A-0525", "A-0625", "A-0700", "A-0800", "A-0900", "A-1000", "A-1150", "B-0200", "B-0450"],
    "hour": 660
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A showed traffic consistent with low-communication training on days 12 and 16. Meridian’s account and registry entry can help distinguish a genuine batch inference job with results delivered on day 19 from training recorded as batch inference.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Meridian Analytics"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A showed GPU-to-GPU traffic consistent with low-communication training on two days with no scheduled job. Probe several GPUs across A’s scheduled pool during day 26 to test whether that traffic accompanies training or inference. Operator-selected samples can help classify the chips they reach, while run size, duration, and coordination remain open.",
  "action": "chip_probe",
  "args": {
    "nodes": ["A-0550", "A-0700", "A-0850", "A-1000", "A-1150"],
    "hour": 610
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Two periodic lines appeared on F1 during the overlap. Check day 7, when only Tidewater's fine-tune was logged. Finding just one of the two lines would support separate runs, especially if the other line also appears on a day when only Ostara's job was logged.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 7
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Meridian’s registry entry corroborates that it is an established company. Its silence leaves the workload unverified. I’ll sample Cluster A on day 19 to see whether the GPU traffic seen on days 12 and 16 persisted through the outbound spike. A change would help locate a workload transition. A similar reading would support sustained low-communication training, while leaving the source of the outbound traffic and the identity of the run unresolved.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 19
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A showed about 10 MB/s per GPU on days 12, 16 and 19, including the day of the outbound spike. Both low-communication training and batch inference with a final bulk export remain possible. I’ll check F1 on day 12 for a periodic power signature at the start of the allocation. A line would add evidence alongside the tap reading, though it would leave the workload and its continuity unresolved.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 12
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The five scheduled-pool GPUs presented at hour 610 read idle, although the independent tap detected traffic inside Cluster A on day 26 consistent with low-communication training. Probe across A’s listed interactive pool at the same hour to test for training under an inference label. The readings can classify the chips presented, while run size and duration still need corroboration.",
  "action": "chip_probe",
  "args": {
    "nodes": ["A-0025", "A-0075", "A-0125", "A-0175", "A-0225", "A-0275", "A-0325", "A-0375", "A-0425", "A-0475"],
    "hour": 610
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 28 probe found inference or idle readings on the chips presented. Cluster A's tap reading still fits low-communication training, and the probe covered only a few GPUs at one hour. Check F1's power spectrum for independent evidence of a periodic workload on day 28. A line could also come from batch inference or cooling, and an absent line would leave variable training possible.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 28
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Inference remains plausible for the chips presented, while Cluster A’s elevated peer traffic could reflect unlogged low-communication training. The declared fine-tunes could be separate runs, and the inventory and escrow checks found no discrepancy in the records they cover. Sample the A–B links on day 26 to test whether the unexplained traffic was confined to Cluster A or extended across clusters.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A-B",
    "day": 26
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The two Cluster A allocations total 50,400 GPU-hours if combined. Each customer confirmed its own order and paid from its own account. The independent power sensor found one periodic line in each solo window and two during the overlap, supporting two separate fine-tunes. The aggregate tap reading is consistent with either explanation.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "NO_VIOLATION",
    "confidence": 93,
    "integrity": "CLEAN",
    "finding": "The evidence supports two separate 25,200 GPU-hour fine-tunes on Cluster A and a 24,800 GPU-hour fine-tune on Cluster B.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "F1 showed one line near 0.44 Hz on day 7, two near 0.39 and 0.54 Hz on day 8, and one near 0.59 Hz on day 10. The pattern tracks the solo and overlap windows.",
        "source": "power_spectrum",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier's own sensor"
      },
      {
        "what": "Tidewater Robotics and Ostara Legal had longstanding registrations at their own addresses, confirmed distinct fine-tuning orders, and paid from accounts in their own names.",
        "source": "customer_check and bank_payments",
        "vouched_by": "Company register, customers, and bank"
      },
      {
        "what": "All 30 daily logs were escrowed and match the current records. Declared workloads and inventory passed the fixed energy, outbound traffic, billing, and inventory checks.",
        "source": "log_escrow and balance_checks",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier's own archive and fixed reconciliation program"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Reviewed days 1–30 for both listed clusters using sealed logs and utility data for all three feeds. Direct measurements covered F1 on days 7, 8, and 10 and aggregate traffic inside Cluster A on day 8. Traffic between the two allocations and direct workload measurements on Cluster B were unavailable in the evidence reviewed.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The F1 load increase on days 6–11 matches the two logged fine-tunes. Separate orders, payments, and power signatures support treating them as distinct runs. The day 8 traffic supports training activity but cannot establish coordination. The F2 load increase on days 17–19 matches the logged 24,800 GPU-hour fine-tune. The inventory matched by count, all daily logs were present, and all invoices were paid."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Three F1 spectrum checks found no periodic line. The tap readings still fit low-communication training, and the day 19 outbound spike could be a batch export. I’ll ask staff for a concrete account of both observations and a reference I can verify independently.",
  "action": "staff",
  "args": {
    "question": "For Meridian Analytics job bt-0431, what operation generated the sustained GPU-to-GPU traffic, did the workload update model weights, and what caused the roughly 40 TB of extra outbound traffic on day 19? Please identify any customer-side or third-party record that can confirm the workload or the transfer."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 26 A–B traffic is consistent with low-communication training. Check the same link on day 12, when Cluster A also showed elevated peer traffic and the scheduler listed no job. Recurrence would support an unlogged cross-cluster workload over a one-day transfer, while leaving continuity, GPU count, and run identity open. Separate fine-tunes and batch inference remain plausible for the declared jobs. Inventory and escrow checks found no shipment-count discrepancy or missing daily copies.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A-B",
    "day": 12
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The grid records show nine days of elevated load on F1 and two on F2. Either period could reflect training, batch inference, or several independent jobs. Check the sealed scheduler logs for recorded allocations, overlaps, and gaps to guide independent measurements of workload type and coordination.",
  "action": "log_escrow",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A’s sampled traffic on days 12 and 28 fits low-communication training, but the day 28 probes and power spectrum did not corroborate training. Check the A–B links on day 28. Elevated GPU traffic would support investigating a shared workload across the clusters. Inference-level traffic would weaken that explanation for the listed links while leaving a run within A possible.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A-B",
    "day": 28
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Test four explanations. Separate short fine-tunes would produce traffic tied to their logged windows. Inference could explain the steady load and the live probe readings, though the recurring A–B traffic calls for another workload check. An unlogged coordinated run could produce training-like power signatures on both feeds during days with no scheduled jobs. Unlisted capacity is less likely given the shipment and system counts, and grid readings constrain how much activity could be hidden. Check F2 on day 26, when the independent tap detected elevated A–B traffic. A line would support further investigation, while either result leaves GPU count and run duration unresolved.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F2",
    "day": 26
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The F1 load coincides with a 700-GPU job recorded as batch inference. The sealed copy confirms the label was recorded at the time. Sample traffic within cluster A on day 26 to test whether GPU communication is consistent with batch inference or coordinated training.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 26
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Four explanations guide the remaining checks. Separate short fine-tunes can explain the logged power increases and can be checked against independent tap readings during their scheduled windows. Inference could explain the baseline load and the first five interactive-pool probe readings, so sample the five requested chips the operator did not present. An unlogged coordinated run could explain the recurring A–B traffic, so look for training on those chips and check Cluster B next if the sample is inconclusive. Unlisted capacity is another possibility, constrained by the matching shipment and system counts and the grid readings. The tap and power spectra so far leave GPU count, duration, and run identity unresolved.",
  "action": "chip_probe",
  "args": {
    "nodes": ["A-0275", "A-0325", "A-0375", "A-0425", "A-0475"],
    "hour": 610
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Bank records confirm Meridian paid for the job. Staff attribute the day 19 outbound spike to a bulk download, while the optical tap still measured about 10 MB/s per GPU that day. I’ll check F1 for a periodic signal on day 19 to test whether a training-like power signature appeared during the reported export. A line could also come from batch inference.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 19
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "All ten sampled A interactive GPUs read as inference at hour 610. The recurring A–B traffic could reflect unlogged low-communication training or atypical inference. Probe both B pools at the same hour to test for training on the other side of those links. The declared short fine-tunes could be separate jobs, which can be checked against tap readings during their logged windows. Unlisted capacity remains possible, though shipment counts and metered energy agree with the site list. Missing or revised logs are less likely because all 30 sealed copies match the current records. A training-like probe reading would identify a candidate workload, while GPU count, duration, and run identity would still need corroboration.",
  "action": "chip_probe",
  "args": {
    "nodes": ["B-0100", "B-0300", "B-0450", "B-0600", "B-0750"],
    "hour": 610
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 27 probe found training signatures on four sampled GPUs, and the optical tap found training-scale traffic on days 20 and 26. Check F1 on day 26 for a periodic signature that can be compared across the nine-day load plateau. That comparison can help distinguish a sustained coordinated run from several shorter or overlapping workloads.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {"feed": "F1", "day": 26}
}

{
  "thinking": "Independent taps found elevated A–B traffic on days 12 and 26, when the scheduler shows no jobs. The live chip samples leave both atypical inference and unlogged low-communication training plausible. Ask which workload and GPU groups generated the traffic so the remaining two points can fund a targeted independent check.",
  "action": "staff",
  "args": {
    "question": "What workload generated the elevated GPU-to-GPU traffic between A and B on days 12 and 26? Identify the node ranges on both clusters, when the workload started and stopped, and where it was recorded. If it is still running, identify five active GPUs you can present for a live probe."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Both customers independently described buying interactive inference, which offers a plausible explanation for their absence from the scheduled-job log. The day 28 tap still found above-typical inference traffic within A and across A–B. The earlier probe never reached B. Probe B at the same hour to test whether the presented chips show training activity. Inference or idle readings would weaken that explanation for those chips at that hour.",
  "action": "chip_probe",
  "args": {
    "nodes": ["B-0200", "B-0450", "B-0550", "B-0650", "B-0750"],
    "hour": 660
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Independent measurements establish sustained load and unusually high GPU-to-GPU traffic on Cluster A. Both low-communication training and the reported batch-labeling workload remain plausible. The available checks cannot establish whether one training process exceeded 50,000 GPU-hours.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "UNDETERMINED",
    "confidence": 95,
    "integrity": "LIMITED",
    "finding": "Meridian’s 134,400-GPU-hour allocation could contain an oversized training run, but the evidence does not establish its workload or whether it was one coordinated process.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "The scheduler lists 700 GPUs on Cluster A for 192 hours under one Meridian batch job. All 30 scheduler copies match the sealed archive, which attests to what was recorded.",
        "source": "scheduler_log and log_escrow",
        "vouched_by": "Operator for job details and verifier archive for the match"
      },
      {
        "what": "Cluster A averaged about 10 MB/s per GPU on days 12, 16 and 19, consistent with low-communication training and roughly 1,000 times the stated inference baseline.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier optical tap"
      },
      {
        "what": "F1 drew about 18 MWh per day on days 12–19, up from about 8 MWh. F1 showed no periodic line on days 12, 14, 16 or 19.",
        "source": "utility_energy and power_spectrum",
        "vouched_by": "Grid operator and verifier power sensor"
      },
      {
        "what": "Outbound traffic stayed near baseline through day 18 and rose by about 40 TB on day 19. Meridian is an established registered company and paid EUR 268,800. It did not reply during the audit.",
        "source": "outbound_traffic, customer_check and bank_payments",
        "vouched_by": "Internet provider, company register and bank"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Reviewed shipments and the site list, all 30 days of scheduler and escrow records, daily grid energy on F0–F2, and site-wide outbound traffic. Direct workload measurements covered only sampled links within A on days 12, 16 and 19 and F1 spectra on days 12, 14, 16 and 19. B and A–B links were untapped. No chip probe covered Meridian’s historical allocation because it ended before the live audit window.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The ISP’s daily data localized the outbound surplus to one day. Staff attributed it to a bulk download of labeling results, but the site-wide records could not verify the transfer’s owner or contents. The early outbound shortfall reflects near-baseline egress and remains compatible with both training and batch work that stores output locally. Elevated peer traffic remained unexplained by staff. Four spectra without a periodic line weigh against regularly periodic training, while intermittent or irregular training remains possible. The brief F2 load increase matches the recorded 7,200-GPU-hour Ostara fine-tune. Shipment counts, escrow copies and bank records showed no discrepancies in the inventory, log-retention and payment checks."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The independent tap found elevated traffic inside A and between A and B on day 26, when the scheduler shows no job. Staff did not identify the workload. Check A’s power feed on that same day for a second signal that could help distinguish low-communication training from atypical inference. A periodic line would strengthen the training hypothesis, though the GPU count, duration, and run identity would remain unresolved.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 26
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "All six recorded fine-tunes share a time window. They could be independent jobs or parts of one coordinated run. Sample cross-cluster GPU traffic on day 26, when both feeds show sustained elevated load. Heavy traffic would support coordination across clusters, while low traffic would leave coordination within either cluster open.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A-B",
    "day": 26
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Separate short fine-tunes fit the recorded load increases. Atypical inference could explain the sampled chip readings and steady load. Unlogged low-communication training could explain the recurring cross-cluster traffic. Unlisted capacity is less likely given the inventory and energy checks. Missing daily logs and late payments are unsupported by the escrow and billing checks. The independent measurements leave the workload type, run identity, and GPU-hours unresolved.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "UNDETERMINED",
    "confidence": 95,
    "integrity": "LIMITED",
    "finding": "Recurring cross-cluster traffic on days with no logged jobs remains unexplained, and the evidence cannot establish whether any single training run exceeded 50,000 GPU-hours.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "On days 12 and 26, sampled peer traffic was 0.01–0.02 GB/s within A and about 0.02 GB/s between A and B, well above typical inference and consistent with low-communication training.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's optical tap"
      },
      {
        "what": "No periodic line was detected in F1 on day 12 or in F1 or F2 on day 26.",
        "source": "power_spectrum",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's power sensor"
      },
      {
        "what": "At hour 610, the 20 GPUs presented across A and B read as inference or idle.",
        "source": "chip_probe",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's cable-current probe"
      },
      {
        "what": "All 30 sealed daily copies matched the current scheduler logs.",
        "source": "log_escrow",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's sealed archive"
      },
      {
        "what": "The manufacturer recorded 2,000 GPUs delivered, matching the site list. Energy, outbound traffic, billing, and declared job-size checks passed.",
        "source": "inventory, system_list, balance_checks",
        "vouched_by": "chip manufacturer, operator, and fixed program over the records"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Reviewed days 1–30 for both listed clusters through daily feed energy, site outbound traffic, and scheduler records. Independent taps sampled links within A and between A and B on days 12 and 26. Spectra covered F1 on days 12 and 26 and F2 on day 26. Chip probes covered operator-presented GPUs in both clusters at hour 610 only. There was no independent workload classification during declared job windows, continuous run-level measurement, inspection of unlisted links, or customer verification.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The F1 increases on days 4–6 and the F2 increases on days 9–10 fit the two declared 7,200 GPU-hour fine-tunes. The F1 and outbound increases on days 15–16 fit the declared 9,600 GPU-hour batch job. The recurring peer traffic on days 12 and 26 remains unexplained. Staff did not identify its source, and the sampled spectra and chip readings leave both atypical inference and unlogged low-communication training possible. Inventory, escrow, and billing checks found no discrepancy in the records they covered."
  }
